From e3e94a253c6637a6fcfee0d33d5879e2bd01eb99 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 01:09:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 163/164] media: rkisp1: Add a MIPI additional-data capture
 video node

The MIPI receiver can divert CSI-2 long packets whose (VC, DT) matches
one of its four additional-data selectors into a small FIFO, entirely
separate from the image path. This is the only out-of-band data path
this ISP generation has - later Rockchip ISPs grew a DMA engine for it.
The IMX258 delivers its shield-pixel (PDAF) samples as such packets,
with data type 0x2f, during line blanking. The driver defines the
registers and unmasks the overflow interrupt, but never programs the
selectors, so the FIFO stays unused.

Add a rkisp1_addata metadata capture node that delivers one buffer per
frame: a small header (flags, packet count, byte counts) followed by the
verbatim FIFO content. Hardware probing on the PinePhone Pro established
the FIFO framing - each packet appears as its 4-byte MIPI long-packet
header followed by the dword-padded payload - and the kernel walks those
headers only to end the buffer on a whole packet and to detect desync;
the payload semantics stay in userspace, because the shield-pixel layout
is undocumented and still being reverse engineered.

The same probing measured the FIFO at 2048 dwords and established the
rule the drain design follows: the FIFO may only be popped while the
sensor readout is quiet. A pop issued during readout stalls the
register bus and hard-wedges the machine - the popping CPU freezes on
the unretired load, immune even to pseudo-NMIs, and other cores follow
as they touch the wedged interconnect. That is independent of the
additional-data interrupts (a 1 ms timer drain wedged identically with
all of them masked; conversely the FIFO can overflow and sit full
indefinitely with nothing reading it, the overflow interrupt firing a
single clean edge). Pops any time from the MIPI frame-end interrupt
onward are stable; the capture is drained in one bounded until-empty
loop from the later ISP frame interrupt, for margin and because the
shared handler runs it before the MIPI ISR, so the capture also closes
on the frame it belongs to. The overflow interrupt only counts and
flags the loss.

The FIFO depth is thus a hard cap of 8 KB on the data recoverable per
frame: userspace must size the sensor's AF window so a frame's output
fits (an IMX258 5% window emits ~6 KB and ~1500 usable phase samples
per frame), or accept the hardware-truncated capture the buffer flags
report. The hardware's fill-level machinery cannot lift the cap: its
threshold (a byte count, dword-aligned - the 13-bit field spans exactly
the FIFO; measured, the TRM's "level in dwords" is only the
granularity) compares against a level that only a mid-readout drain
could keep down, and its interrupt is a latching level condition that
storms if ever unmasked.

There is no DMA on this path, so the drain is a bounded loop of
register reads in hard interrupt context; the per-frame cost scales
with how much data the sensor is configured to emit, which is
userspace's choice, as is the per-frame capture limit
(fmt.meta.buffersize, clamped to 8 KB - 1 MB). The actual amount
captured each frame is variable and reported in bytesused and in the
header.

The selectors are programmed from the sensor's get_frame_desc() - every
CSI-2 entry whose data type differs from the image stream's - and only
while the capture node is streaming; otherwise they are parked at their
match-nothing reset value so the FIFO stays empty. An add_data_dt module
parameter overrides the frame descriptor for experiments (it also serves
sensors that do not implement get_frame_desc() yet).

The additional-data overflow interrupt is excluded from the mipi_error
accounting, which used to count any status other than exactly FRAME_END
as an error. Debugfs gains capture counters and a MIPI register dump
(minus the FIFO register, since reading it pops data); CUR_DATA_ID in
that dump is the cheapest way to see whether a sensor's out-of-band
packets reach the receiver at all.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
